            "A monitor nobody knows how to respond to is worse than no monitor, because it teaches the team to close alerts without reading them. Every model signal therefore needs three things attached before it is switched on: a named owner, a severity that says whether it warrants a page or a ticket, and a runbook giving the checks to run and the actions available. Machine learning systems are also frequently owned by people who do not carry the pager for them, and settling that question in advance is not paperwork; it is the difference between a fifteen-minute response and a three-day one.",
            "The triage order follows directly from the four things called drift, cheapest and most likely first. Start with data quality: check feature freshness, null and default-substitution rates, and whether the upstream jobs ran, and look at the shape of the signal in time, since a step change points at a pipeline and a slope points at the world. Next check calibration alone by comparing predicted rates against observed ones, because if the ranking still holds you can refit the calibration mapping in hours without touching the model. Then check input distributions on the features the model actually weights heavily. Only after those three do you reach concept drift, which needs outcomes grouped by cohort and is therefore the slowest thing to confirm as well as the most serious.",
            "The actions available form a ladder, and the discipline is to take the smallest step that resolves the problem. Adjusting a threshold is fast, versioned, and reversible. Refitting calibration is a few hours and touches nothing else. Rolling back to the previous release undoes a bad change immediately. The kill switch drops to a rule-based fallback when the model itself is the problem. Retraining sits at the bottom because it is the slowest, and it is also the most tempting first suggestion in the room, which is exactly why the ladder should be written down before the incident rather than debated during one.",
            "Alerts need their own maintenance or the whole apparatus decays into noise. Derive thresholds from historical variation instead of from round numbers, so a signal that always swings 15 percent on Mondays does not page anyone. Page only for conditions that require action within the hour and file everything else as a ticket. And measure the alerts themselves: track what fraction of firings led to any action at all, and retune or delete the ones that never do. An alert with 5 percent precision is not a safety net, it is a reason the next real alert gets ignored.",
            "Close each incident by changing the monitoring rather than only the model. Record time to detect and time to mitigate separately, because for model incidents the first is usually dominated by label delay and the second by how far down the ladder you had to go, and those two numbers get improved by completely different work. Every incident should leave behind either one new monitor or one deleted one.",
